Z||000000||Wannabe||04-04-2001||07:48 PM||||Someone local is selling a low mileage 3800 engine out of a 1990 Bonneville for $150 (Canadian, that's like $90US!) I still have to verify the condition of the engine, but was wondering, can anybody confirm that the 1990 engine was 175 hp and 200 lb ft of torque. Also, any idea if the the updated series II intake manifold and tb can be installed on the earlier engine?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||04-04-2001||09:16 PM||||Those hp/tq numbers sound about right, maybe a bit high. But if you want a series II engine, buy a series II engine. The intakes are not interchangeable. There are many more differences than just a better intake.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||04-05-2001||12:06 PM||||&gt;There are many more differences than just a better intake.<P>  That is entirely correct.  The series II 3800 has numerous differences over the 3800, all of which (IMO) make it the choice of preference.<P>&gt;selling a low mileage 3800 engine out of a 1990 Bonneville for $150 <P>  Well, at that price they might as well just give it to you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  But how low milage is this 11 year old motor anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||04-05-2001||02:20 PM||||The main differences in buying a used Series 1 compared to a Series 2 is about 10 hp and $300<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||04-05-2001||05:37 PM||||Sounds like a pass. Having lots of options is good, but it can get complicated. I guess that weighing pros and cons is all part of the fun. About the low milage, my mom had an '89 Buick LeSabre (3800 series 1). She sold it last year with only about 35,000 miles on the clock....you never know until you check it out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
